WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.840
Hey everyone James Murphy here, this&nbsp;
is mCoding, and let's just jump into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.800
it with some controversial events&nbsp;
happening in the world of Python.
&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.040
If you haven't already heard the news,&nbsp;
the CPython developers just pushed out a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.120
breaking change to the language, modifying&nbsp;
longstanding behavior of how int-to-string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.600
and string-to-int conversions work, putting a&nbsp;
default limit on how many digits are allowed.
&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.360
Previously, there was no limit on&nbsp;
the size of integer you could use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.360
as long as you have enough memory to hold it.&nbsp;
This was one of the cool features of Python&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.920
that new programmers would often explore. Just&nbsp;
print math.factorial(10000) and there it is.
&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.000
But if you download Python today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.800
instead you'll be greeted with an error&nbsp;
telling you the answer is too big.
&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.600
The default limit is 4300 digits.
Users must now call this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.040
sys.set_int_max_str_digits to increase the digit&nbsp;
limit or set it to 0 to disable the limit.
&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.080
Now it prints just as before.
This limit only applies to conversions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.760
from int to string and string to int that&nbsp;
aren't in a base that's a power of 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.880
so printing binary and hex are not affected. 
But humans normally write numbers in base 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.080
so this does affect the most common use case.
Also note that this is not a limit on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.920
size of integers outright, only on&nbsp;
conversions to and from strings.
&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.240
So you can still operate on big numbers without&nbsp;
any changes, you just might not be able to print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.280
the results. That's why the error here is on line&nbsp;
4 where the print statement is, and not on line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.400
3 where the answer is actually computed.
Part of what makes this so controversial was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.200
that this change, which undoubtedly breaks some&nbsp;
existing projects, was pushed out with essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.680
no warning to the community not just to the&nbsp;
latest development branch of Python 3.11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.400
but to Python 3.7, 3.8, 3.9, and&nbsp;
3.10 as well in a patch release.
&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.960
Patch releases are generally meant to be&nbsp;
made of small changes that are completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.520
backwards compatible or that fix outright bugs,&nbsp;
errors, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.
&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.120
As such, tooling such as automated test&nbsp;
runners may use the latest patch by default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.360
and package managers may ignore the patch number&nbsp;
when considering version compatibility.
&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.080
This means that projects depending on the old&nbsp;
behavior will have their automated tests broken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.360
and their users who are on the latest&nbsp;
patch will immediately start experiencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.960
these breaking changes, even if the&nbsp;
project hasn't made any new release.
&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.240
So why would they push out a&nbsp;
breaking change like this?
&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.520
Going to the CPython source, this GitHub issue&nbsp;
claims the previous behavior of int/string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.760
conversion is a security vulnerability, which&nbsp;
explains why it was able to be pushed out so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.280
quickly even though it was a breaking change.
This vulnerability was initially reported in May&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.280
of 2020 and officially went public on September&nbsp;
1, 2022, according to RedHat's CVE page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.560
although the GitHub issue was apparently&nbsp;
created a month before, in August.
&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.720
So what is this vulnerability?
Well, CPython uses an N^2 algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.240
in the number of digits for int/string&nbsp;
conversions when the bases aren't compatible.
&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.200
Here, a mere million and one digits&nbsp;
took 4.5 seconds to complete.
&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.600
We can confirm this N^2 behavior empirically by&nbsp;
looking at the timing plots for conversions.
&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.840
We can clearly see superlinear&nbsp;
growth in this plot.
&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.560
And, if we look at a log-log plot and compute&nbsp;
the least squares fit we see that the estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.160
power is very close to 2.
So, empirically at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.080
this really is an N^2 algorithm.
Under the hood, the actual algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.440
that Python uses is very similar to&nbsp;
these basic conversion functions.
&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.480
It's really not much more than repeated division&nbsp;
or multiplication depending on the direction.
&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.560
These may appear to be just O(N) algorithms, but&nbsp;
it's actually O(N^2) because multiplication and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.120
division are not constant-time operations since&nbsp;
we are working with arbitrarily large numbers.
&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.960
So these divide-by-10 or multiply-by-10&nbsp;
are actually O(N) operations themselves.
&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.520
This means that converting between integers&nbsp;
and strings is more expensive than you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.200
expect and it opens Python up to a&nbsp;
denial of service attack on any code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.680
that tries to convert between them.
In particular, any kind of parser or API&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.240
that takes base 10 values as strings may be&nbsp;
vulnerable, including the builtin json module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.920
parsing libraries like Pydantic, and&nbsp;
API frameworks that depend on these.
&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.200
A bad actor can send a moderately large number&nbsp;
with say a million digits to cause an API to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.240
freeze for around 5 seconds like we saw.
The new default digit limit fixes this issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.840
by raising an exception that your code can&nbsp;
catch unless you have explicitly opted-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.240
to allowing this expensive conversion.
Of course, primarily in favor of the change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.080
there are operators and users of services&nbsp;
that have these vulnerable APIs.
&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.760
The original GitHub issue argues that the change&nbsp;
is necessary in part because of how incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.160
common it is to take unsanitized user input from&nbsp;
the internet and pass it directly to int.
&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.720
On the other side are authors and users&nbsp;
of libraries that use big numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.000
meaning this is probably going to&nbsp;
hit math libraries the hardest.
&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.560
This may complicate things for their users and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.440
of course, now the code that they've&nbsp;
already put out there is broken.
&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.560
Some in this camp have argued that doing&nbsp;
anything with unsanitized user input is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.400
already a vulnerability waiting to happen,&nbsp;
so it should really be up to vulnerable APIs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.400
to sanitize their inputs, not the Python&nbsp;
language to limit how numbers work.
&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.240
Others are confused why we're using&nbsp;
these N^2 algorithm, when there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.720
known faster algorithms readily available.
Potentially, switching to faster algorithms could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.640
make the denial of service infeasible, eliminating&nbsp;
the need for the digit limit at all.
&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.120
And then, of course, there are a large number of&nbsp;
people that are perfectly fine with the change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.360
as they are most likely not affected by it,&nbsp;
but just upset by the lack of transparency or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.600
opportunity to have their opinions considered&nbsp;
and to potentially prepare for the break.
	
&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.840
Regardless of how this issue pans out,&nbsp;
the core dev team did apologize for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.280
the lack of transparency on the issue,&nbsp;
although it is sometimes necessary for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.760
security vulnerabilities.
And they are welcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.280
further discussion on the issue.
If you have an opinion on the matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.120
especially if you are affected by the change, the&nbsp;
correct place to respectfully voice your opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.600
is the Python discourse, linked below.
Please keep your suggestions and criticism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.840
constructive and don't use&nbsp;
the discourse to rant.
&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.760
And that's where I pass it off to you.
What do you think about this breaking change?
&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.480
Is protecting vulnerable APIs that you or I&nbsp;
may unknowingly be using every day worth it?
&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.040
Even if that means some math&nbsp;
libraries are broken?
&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.480
Or should vulnerable code be on&nbsp;
the hook to sanitize its inputs?
&nbsp;

